rlb@hoekstra-uitgeverij.nl (Richard Bos) writes:
> I don't care if people want to play slowly or for many terms; I care
> that the game sometimes rewards mere stubbornness more than
> strategy.

I don't think that's entirely accurate. A fully stubborn, minimally
spoiled player is going to produce nothing but _lots_ of early deaths.

There are cases where game does reward (simple strategy + patience)
seemingly more than (multiple, complex strategies + risk taking). I
have a hard time seeing why I should be bothered by this.

I do understand -- and, halfhearted, agree with -- the idea that one
should need to be more than a one-trick pony to consistently ascend.
I remain unconvinced that either pudding-farming in particular or
patience games in general need to be stopped on that basis. (Do you
know _any_ players who P-farm every game? If so, can they win 12 games
out of 13 consecutive?)

> As for the lack of tension between the mid game and the Wizard,
> that's inescapable in the current game.

The game already has a phase which is a make-every-turn count,
stop-for-nothing sprint. Having mid-game _not_ be like that is
pleasant, and makes for a bit of variety. If I want Quake, or
Warcraft, I know where to find them.

> Creating somewhat stronger monsters isn't the solution, either;
> preventing the player from turning into a walking tank is.

s/player/character/

And, what's the problem, if someone wants to do that? If I need to be
X buff to ascend, and don't start my run until I'm 10X, that's
wasteful and perhaps a bit silly, but where's the harm?

"Nethack isn't AD&D" means, in part, that "munchkin" needn't be
pejorative.

